import Foundation

extension CapabilityRegistry {
    static let composerAndServers: [CapabilityDefinition] = [
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateBoard, area: "delegate", title: "A machine's dispatcher is a board",
            spec:
                "Each server may run the delegate daemon beside its agent (port 4100, trusting the tailnet the way the bridges do, so a password is asked for only when the daemon is set to ask): a tiered task dispatcher that takes a packet, lets a cheaper model try it in an isolated worktree, judges it with the packet's verifier, escalates a failure up the ladder with the verifier's own words, and applies the passing patch unstaged. The board is where a person meets it: reached from the server's own screen (DelegateEntryPoint.serverRowTitle), from Home and from a chat (delegateHandoff), it leads with the work rather than the connection — the machine and the dispatcher's version are the title's second line (DelegateBoard.subtitle), New packet is the primary action at the top, and a host is picked rather than typed. The tiers are one ladder of connected rungs (DelegateBoardRung): the rung's label, the model answering there by its short name, its health only when that says something (answering, or down and why — never \"not probed\"), and its record across every class folded in (\"93% of 45 · 6.4s\"), with the daemon's promotion hints (DelegatePromotion.hints) under it, because a tier assignment changes only on a streak the table can show. Runs are grouped by what they ask of the reader (DelegateBoard.sections: needs you — a rung to approve or a patch to read — then running, then earlier), each row a DelegateRunRow: the goal as its headline, where it is or how it ended under it (DelegateRunStory.subtitle), the repository and the age, a pill that says where a pass's patch went (Applied, Review, Discarded) or how a run stopped, the run's ladder at the size of a row, and its activity breathing while it works, knocking while it waits for you, and holding still once settled. Live runs fold as their events arrive; the words are Core's and a client draws rows. The demo world's two machines share one scripted dispatcher (DelegateDemo, DelegateDemoServer) behind the same transport as the daemon (DelegateTransport), so the board, the composer, the run and the approval work end to end with no network: a run still working climbs over seconds, one waits for your answer, one had to climb, one ran out of ladder, and a packet you write is tried, fails once where the ladder lets it and passes one rung up. The board wears one standing sentence saying so (DelegateBoard.note)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateComposer, area: "delegate", title: "A packet is written, not dictated",
            spec:
                "A new packet asks first for the two things only the person knows — the goal written for a reader with no other context, and the repository on that machine, picked from where this machine's chats work and where its runs went (DelegateRepoChoices) rather than typed — then the paths the worker may write, and folds everything the class already decides into one Plan line (DelegateDraft.planSummary: class, range, verifier) that opens to the class, verifier, ladder, mode, effort, read-first and notes. Review before it lands (DelegateDraft.review) is on by default wherever the daemon can hold a patch (DelegateCapabilities.supportsReview) and says plainly when it cannot. The ladder is one custom control everywhere (DelegateLadder): a rung per tier, tap a rung to start there, drag the cap to set how far the run may climb, each rung labelled with the model it resolves to on that server; unset means the class decides. The words are DelegateComposerWords'; what stops a packet from going (DelegateDraft.problems) and what is merely worth saying before it goes (DelegateDraft.cautions: an open scope, no verifier) are two different things and the form shows the difference. Verifiers worth offering are suggested from what the paths look like (DelegateDraft.verifySuggestions). Sending starts the run and lands on its story; the packet is the daemon's, kept in the repository's own .delegate/packets as that repository's regression set."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateRun, area: "delegate", title: "One run is a story with a ladder",
            spec:
                "A run's screen is one DelegateRunReading: the goal as its headline and the facts in one line under it (class, range, mode, repository, duration, tokens); then the lead — the one thing that matters now, before anything else: why it stopped with the verifier's own last lines set as output, the rung waiting for you, the patch ready for review, or where an applied patch went; then the ladder — the rung it is on lit, the ones it failed marked, the ones beyond its ceiling greyed (DelegateRungState), models by their short names; then exactly one primary action with the rest beside it (DelegateRunAction: approve, apply, climb or run again — hold, discard, cancel, a cheaper rung, a copy, and Run again on… for any rung); then the patch's files; then one timeline in the daemon's own lines (DelegateStoryLine), a failed attempt's verifier tail or out-of-scope files carried under its own line instead of told twice in a second list, a worker's progress shown only while the run is out. The past is read from the daemon's stored events and the present streams in on the same stream — including what happens after a run ends, a held patch applied or discarded — so reopening a run finds it exactly where it is. Nothing here commits a patch, because a patch nobody reviewed on the machine that has to live with it is not a commit."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateApproval, area: "delegate", title: "A gated rung asks before it spends",
            spec:
                "In conserve mode the daemon stops before the tier it was told to ask about and waits. The wait is a state with a face: the run's activity knocks (ActivityKind.needsApproval), its ladder holds the rung as held, its row says which rung it is waiting on, and the run screen offers exactly two answers — approve, which climbs, and hold, which ends the run as held (DelegateClient.approve). The request survives the screen: a run left waiting is still waiting when the board is reopened, read from the stored approval_required event, and a decision made from another device is seen as approval_resolved rather than as silence."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateProGate, area: "delegate", title: "Delegation is part of Pro, its quietest perk",
            spec:
                "On a client that sells Pro, delegation is part of it and its least prominent selling point — listed last among the perks (ProOffer.perks and the phone's sheet alike), never the general sheet's lead. Every road into the board — the server row, the Home entry, the menu — leads a free copy to the Pro sheet with the delegate pitch (DelegateProGate.pitch, the perk listed in ProOffer.perks) rather than to a half of the feature, and a Pro copy straight to the board. The gate is DelegateProGate.allows(isPro:sells:demo:): a client that sells nothing gates nothing, so the direct Mac build and Linux are simply whole, and the demo world is never behind the price, so App Review and a first-run user work the whole feature on the scripted machines. Nothing nags: the gate is touched only by a person walking through it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .betaMark, area: "settings", title: "A feature in beta says so, and the mark is one thing everywhere",
            spec:
                "Every feature that is in beta is a case of BetaFeature and answers one BetaCard — name, symbol and glyph, a title, a lead, the paragraphs that say why it is in beta, what is solid, what is still rough, what changed since a release, and where its log lives — so no client spells a beta word (BetaWords holds the shared ones). The mark is a capsule beside the feature's title (BETA) drawn as an invitation rather than a warning, and BetaWords.marked puts it in front of a line that already says something; it opens the card on the gesture the platform has for asking — hovering on a desktop is enough, a popover that stays while the pointer is on it and leaves when the pointer does; tapping on the phone, a sheet sized to its words. The mark has a freshness read from BetaSeenStore (one key per feature holding the version its card was last opened at): unseen, changed when the card has something newer than the version last seen, or settled. A fresh mark is announced once by a single pass of light across the capsule (BetaSheen, position from absolute time, about 1.4 seconds, nothing at all under reduced motion) and a changed card leads with Since you last looked; a settled mark holds perfectly still, and opening the card marks it seen. Nothing nags: no badge dot, no modal, no repeat. The card is the title with the mark, the lead, the paragraphs, two short lists (Solid, Still rough) and two actions: Copy report, which puts BetaReport.compose — the feature, the app and system, and the lines the feature's own surface supplies — on the pasteboard and nowhere else, and Got it. A screen reader hears BetaCard.spoken and the two lists as lists. A feature leaves beta by its case being deleted, never hidden, so every client loses the mark together."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateClassDefaults, area: "delegate", title: "The class decides, and the composer says what it decided",
            spec:
                "A packet that leaves a field blank is filled from the daemon's own class table, and the composer says so before it goes instead of leaving the ladder reading \"the class decides\". The daemon lists its policies on the capabilities (DelegateCapabilities.classPolicies, modePolicies, from delegate 0.2.0; empty from an older daemon); picking a class fills a verifier that was blank or was only ever the previous class's own default (DelegateDraft.choose), and one sentence under the ladder resolves the whole run the way the daemon will — where it starts, how far it may climb, whose choice each was, and what conserve or rush does to it, including the rung it will ask about (DelegateDraftPlan.legend). Every rung of the composer's ladder wears what the numbers say about it for that class — a pass rate and an average, or untried (DelegateBoard.rungNote, DelegateRung.note) — so a start is chosen on evidence rather than a hunch. The demo's dispatcher carries policies of its own so the sentence can be read without a machine."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateNextSteps, area: "delegate", title: "A settled run says what to do next",
            spec:
                "A run that is over offers its next moves by name rather than a bare tier picker (DelegateRunStory.nextSteps): after a pass, the same packet one rung down — a pass there is the streak the table promotes on; after a stop, one rung above where it stopped and the rung it stopped on; always, a new packet that opens the composer filled from this one's goal, paths and verifier (DelegateDraft(packet:)), editable before it goes. The general replay-on-any-rung road stays beside them. Every title and every second line is Core's; a client draws rows or buttons."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateSetup, area: "delegate", title: "A machine with no dispatcher is shown the road",
            spec:
                "A real machine whose board is not answering carries the two commands that make it answer (DelegateSetup.steps, DelegateSetup.isWanted): install delegate with cargo, then write its config and start it as a user service that trusts the tailnet, so nothing is pasted back — each row copyable with one gesture and each with a line saying what the command does. The demo machines never show it, and a machine that answers never does either; the board is the first place a person meets the daemon and it must not be a dead end that names a port."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateNotices, area: "delegate", title: "A run you follow taps your shoulder",
            spec:
                "A run this device follows — one it started, or one it opened while running — raises a notice the moment a gated rung waits for a person or the run ends without one (DelegateDesk.didNotice, DelegateRunStory.notice): Delegate needs you, ready for your review (a pass whose patch is held), passed at a rung, or failed, with the packet's headline as the body. A hold or a cancel is the person's own doing and says nothing, and a stream reopened on a run replays its past without raising it again (DelegateDesk.isRecent). Each client raises it through its own notifier under the existing switches — a wait under the approvals switch, an end under the turn-complete switch — and never while the person is looking at the app."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateReview, area: "delegate", title: "A passing patch waits to be read",
            spec:
                "A run asked to review (DelegateOverrides.review, sent as apply: review to delegate 0.4+, and asked for by default from every client) holds its passing patch instead of applying it: the run ends passed, its delivery reads pending (DelegateRun.delivery, DelegateRunStory.delivery), its pill says Review, it moves to the board's Needs you group and knocks there, and a notice says it is ready for review. The run's screen leads with it: whether a verifier judged it, the files with their line counts once the patch is read (DelegateClient.patch, DelegatePatch.files, DelegateFileRow), each file opening its own diff through the same gutter the repository surface draws (GitPatchReader), and exactly two answers: Apply to the tree — unstaged, after git apply --check, refused with git's own reason and nothing touched when the tree moved under it — and Discard, which the tree never sees while the patch stays readable. Applying while a chat is working in the same repository asks first and says which chat (DelegateApplyCheck). Either answer is recorded on the run as an event after its end and as its delivery, so every device reads the same outcome. A daemon too old to hold a patch applies as it always did and the composer says so; the demo dispatcher holds its runs for review with hand-written patches so the whole road works with no machine."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .delegateHandoff, area: "delegate", title: "A chat hands a task to the dispatcher",
            spec:
                "A task usually arises inside a conversation, so a chat is a door into the composer: /delegate (SlashDispatch.delegatePreflight, contributed to the catalog by the app as CommandCatalogStore.delegateCommand, with the words after it as the goal) and a menu item in the chat's own menu (DelegateHandoff.menuTitle) both open the packet composer for that chat's machine with the goal written and the repository set to the directory the chat works in (DelegateHandoff), so the two fields hardest to type on a phone arrive already right. The road goes through the same Pro gate as every other door, and sending lands on the run's own screen."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .chooserBriefing, area: "composer", title: "A tab and a door explain themselves",
            spec:
                "Every machine tab in the model chooser wears its state as a colour (ModelMachineState: answering needs no dot, asking is quiet, not answering is danger, a remembered list is attention) and every door knows what kind of door it is (ModelDoorKind: runs on the server, subscription, API key, gateway, free tier — classified from the provider key, local by fact). Holding or right-clicking a chip opens its card (ModelChooser.briefing(machine:)/briefing(door:) → ChooserBriefing): what the server is and where its catalog comes from (opencode lists what that machine configured; Claude Code is a shortlist and runs any id), the state in words with what it means for a pick, what a pick there does, exactly what is behind it — models, families, on its own hardware, which can see pictures or read PDFs or take an effort level, how many are used up — and every door with its count and kind, or for a door its kind explained, what is behind it by family, and how many of its models are also behind another door. The words are Core's; a client draws headings, lines and footnotes. An empty list is one with nothing under any heading, never one whose families are folded shut — the refusal used to be drawn over the very list it was refusing."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .freshCanvas, area: "transcript", title: "A sent prompt rises to the top and the answer streams onto an empty page",
            spec:
                "Sending scrolls the conversation so the prompt just written rests at the top of the window (FreshCanvas.headroom under the edge) and everything under it is empty, so the answer streams onto a blank canvas rather than into the last inch above the composer; the rest of the conversation is one scroll up. The room is made, not found: the client pads the end of the transcript by FreshCanvas.room — read from the page itself (where the prompt stands, how tall the window is, how far the page scrolls without the room already in it) rather than from a tally of the rows, so no gap, inset or estimate can leave it short — and the padding shrinks as the answer grows into it, until the end of the conversation meets the end of the window and ordinary follow-the-bottom takes over. The room stays when the turn ends — a short answer under its question is the page that was asked for, never snapped back to the bottom — and goes only on the next send or on leaving the chat. The move is animated on the display's own clock, one motion from where the reader was to where the prompt lands, never a jump followed by a second correction; a prompt sent from far up the transcript rides the same motion. The rise is kept rather than aimed: the client waits (FreshCanvas.patience) for a row it has just appended to be measurable instead of giving the motion up, and for as long as the canvas holds it checks where the prompt actually stands against the headroom (FreshCanvas.drift/hasLanded) after every layout and moves it back — the room lands a pass after it is asked for, a row measures itself after it is drawn, and the server's own rows replace the ones this device drew, and none of those are visible to a scroll that was aimed once. A hand on the page ends the keeping; the room being taken back is not a hand. Reduced motion lands at once. When the server's row replaces the pending one the prompt does not move, because the swap happens at the offset the reader is already at. A queued message, which has not gone, does not rise — only a send does. The block that rises is the whole send: a picture clipped to the words is docked above them, so the first row of the send is what rests under the top edge, never the words with the picture hidden a row above. The room is measured against what the reader can see, not what is laid out: the live row is laid out in full ahead of its reveal, so the padding shrinks and the canvas lets go by the written height (the live row's unrevealed part comes off the page's end before the room is read), and once it has gone a reader at the bottom is kept on the last written line (FreshCanvas.followOffset) on every frame the reveal advances — the page is pushed up by the writing, never by the layout that ran ahead of it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .vimComposer, area: "composer", title: "Modal composer",
            spec:
                "The composer runs VimEngine: normal mode hides the caret and doubles as the app's normal key context, visual modes work, half-typed commands are protected, unbound keys fall back to vim."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .slashCompletion, area: "composer", title: "Slash command completion",
            spec:
                "Typing / offers the backend's availableCommands through the shared SlashCompletion ranking: the whole word typed out first, then a prefix, then a namespace:name segment matched on its bare half (/planner reaching project:planner), then letters found inside, then letters found in order but apart (/gm reaching git:merge) — and inside a tier, the commands this device reached for most recently (SlashRecents). Every row states what it is: the letters that matched tinted inside the name, the server's own argumentHint as a trailing chip, and the plugin or project that contributed it. The list is keyboard-navigable — arrows or tab to walk, enter or tab to accept, escape to dismiss — without taking those keys from an ordinary draft. Past the command's name the list gives way to that one command's signature and description, held on screen while the argument is written, and a word the catalog does not have says so rather than vanishing under the caret. Every composer the app has answers this, the quick ask included: it is the same prompt box, so / means the same thing in it — offered from the machine's own catalog (fetched with no project, cached per server through CommandCatalogStore so the first keystroke is never blank) minus the commands that read a transcript, since the conversation it mints has none, and a word missing because there is no project says that rather than only that it is missing."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .slashDispatch, area: "composer", title: "A typed command runs",
            spec:
                "A slash command typed out and sent goes exactly where picking it from the list would have sent it, through the shared SlashDispatch.decide: /compact always through its preflight carrying whatever instruction was typed after it, a command the server knows through the command route with its arguments and the model and effort the composer is wearing, and anything the server has never heard of out as the plain words that were written — the server is the authority on its own grammar, and an agent that resolves its own slash grammar from prompt text gets the prompt untouched. A quick ask decides the same way and carries the decision through the mint (QuickAskSend), so a command typed into it runs as a command in the conversation it minted rather than reaching the model as the word it was typed as."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .commandCatalog, area: "composer", title: "Browse every command",
            spec:
                "The whole catalog is browsable, not just completable: grouped by where each command came from (built in, this project, yours, plugins, skills, MCP servers) with its description, scope and argument hint, searchable over names and descriptions through the same shared ranking, and recently used first. Picking one hands it to the composer half-typed when it takes arguments, and runs it when it does not. A completion list cannot teach a name nobody has seen."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .attachments, area: "composer", title: "Attachments",
            spec:
                "Files and images attach via the shared AttachmentIntake (size-capped), show as removable chips, and ride out with send. One trip through the picker takes as many as the send can carry — a question with pictures usually has more than one, and going back through the sheet, the menu and the library for each is the same decision made four times — kept in the order they were picked, capped by count as well as by size because the whole selection rides inside one message, and answered with one line saying how many landed rather than a toast per file. A paste is an attach as much as a paste: the clipboard is read into one shape (ClipboardOffer) and PasteIntake decides what it means, so all three clients agree without deciding anything themselves — files copied in a file manager become chips under their own names, a picture becomes a chip rather than nothing at all, an overlong paste becomes the file it already is, and only words go in as words, at the caret. What the aim cannot be handed is refused by name rather than dropped silently, and the ordinary word paste is handed back to the platform untouched so undo, selection and the system's own behaviour keep working. A phone has no file manager to copy from, so its clipboard offers pictures and words and the surface says so."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .drafts, area: "composer", title: "Nothing typed is ever lost",
            spec:
                "Every prompt box persists what is in it as it is typed, and hands it back on return — not only the chat composer but Home's, the /compact instruction, the goal condition and a free-typed answer to a question. The shared DraftStore keys each box by what it is writing to (DraftScope: a chat by profile and session, Home by the compose target it would start in) so a draft follows its conversation across panes, clients and restarts rather than the window it was typed in. Recording is per keystroke and the write is coalesced onto a quiet moment (DraftStore.quietSeconds), off the caller's thread and into the store's own file — a burst of typing costs one write, and the last word still lands without anyone remembering to save. Clients flush by hand where the process is about to stop being asked (resign, terminate, window or pane close), sending clears the draft immediately so a crash cannot hand a sent prompt back, and the store is bounded by recency so a device that has typed into hundreds of chats never carries a write proportional to all of them."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sendQueue, area: "composer", title: "A queued message is still yours",
            spec:
                "Sending while a turn runs is allowed and visibly queued (Send becomes Queue), and the message goes the moment the turn yields. Where the backend keeps no queue of its own (an older bridge, opencode 1) it is held on the device rather than handed to the server, and that is the version of queueing where the message is still yours: the next thing you type is the thing you most often want to reword, reorder or take back once you have read another paragraph of the answer, and a queue entry on the far side of a wire is one nobody can reach. Every waiting message is therefore a row in the transcript — dimmed, marked, and never drawn as though it had been sent — and it opens: a press on it, or ↑ from an empty composer for the most recent one, puts it back in the box (SendQueueReading.upArrowTakesBack; only from an empty box, because in a half-typed paragraph that key is moving the caret). The entry keeps its place in the order while it is being rewritten and sending replaces it there rather than appending a new one, which is the difference between editing and deleting-and-re-adding; clearing it to nothing is how it is taken back. Nothing drains while a message is open for editing — sending it out from under the person editing it is the one thing the queue exists to prevent — and a send that fails goes back to the head of the queue rather than the tail, so a blip on the connection cannot silently reorder what somebody wrote. The whole model is SendQueue/QueuedSend in Core and no client invents a word of it. The queue belongs to the conversation rather than to the surface showing it: every change is written through to SendQueueStore, keyed by profile and session, so a message queued at midnight is still queued at eight and a process the platform ended did not eat it; a pane or a view model that stops showing a chat hands the queue to the store rather than carrying it into the next conversation — which is how a message written for one chat used to be sent into another — and whoever shows the chat next reads it back. A chat nobody is looking at still drains: the background watch that follows a turn a pane left running sends the next waiting message the moment SendQueueDrain.mayDrain says the turn yielded, and a launch walks the store and wakes a watcher per held conversation, so the queue goes whichever chat is on screen and whether or not the app was."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sharedQueue, area: "composer", title: "One queue for every client of a conversation",
            spec:
                "Where the conversation's backend keeps a queue (BackendCapabilities.supportsServerQueue: claude-bridge and omp-bridge that report queue 1 on /status, opencode 2's inbox) a message written behind a running turn lives on the server, so the queue every client draws is ConversationState.queue in the server's order, and a message queued on the phone is a row on the Mac and the desk too. Every client reads that account through ConversationQueue.display in Core and draws the same rows: the server's items first, then whatever this device still holds, each saying where it is held (QueueReading) and never drawn as though it had been sent. Queueing hands the message to AgentConversation.enqueue under its own UUID, so asking again is always safe, and the device keeps it in SendQueueStore only until the server has it. Nothing on a client drains a message the server holds: the server starts the head when the turn closes. Editing (only where supportsQueueEdit) and cancelling go to the server; an answer of QueueError.alreadyStarted is shown as already sent and the row goes away. A Stop pauses the server's queue, and a non-empty paused queue shows one Resume card beneath its messages (resumeQueue) while an empty one shows nothing. A message written offline, or one the server refused, stays in the store as unsent with its own id, is labelled as not sent yet and is handed over at the tail when the server answers again, retried on a backoff without waiting for a state; messages a version that held everything on the device left in queue.json are handed over oldest first on the first connection to a capable server, and a conversation no pane shows hands over what it holds from the background watch. Where the backend has no server queue, and for a command opencode 2 has no inbox for, the device-local queue and its drain are exactly what they were, and those rows say they are held on this device. What the device drains waits for the server's queue to be empty (SendQueueDrain.mayDrain), so a device-held command never overtakes the messages written before it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelEffortPicker, area: "composer", title: "Model and effort choice",
            spec:
                "The next turn's model and reasoning effort are pickable from availableModels, and the choice rides every road a turn starts on — send(model:reasoningEffort:) for a typed prompt, run(_:arguments:model:reasoningEffort:) for a slash command, a compaction and a goal — because a command is a turn and a server asked for nothing answers on its own default, which is how a chat ends up wearing the name of a model that never answered it. A pick is recorded per session and as the server's last-used default (ModelPreferenceStore/EffortPreferenceStore.recordPick), and a chat opens on initialModel/initialEffort — never a hardcoded default. When the server's session record names a door for its model (AgentSession.modelProviderID — opencode reports one), that door settles how the bare model string resolves against the catalog, so a session on the direct DeepSeek key reopens on the key rather than on the plan's copy of the same model — and a pick with no door at all still resolves its model by id, so a reopened conversation keeps the model's own levels instead of a list that stopped matching. The effort menu lists only what the picked model can run (ModelEffort.options): a level the model cannot take is absent, not dimmed, and a model with no levels has no effort control. A command that pins its own model still wins on the server: the pick is what the client asks for, not what it orders. Claude Code's effort menu includes ultracode, which the server maps to xhigh plus standing workflow orchestration; typing the word in a prompt opts that turn in the same way."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelEffortDial, area: "composer", title: "One dial for model and effort",
            spec:
                "On every client the model and the effort are one decision — which machine, and how hard — so the composer carries one pill for both rather than two dropdowns, and the pill says the heat before the word: a dot in the model family's hue, the model's short word in ink, then the effort word in a slot sized to the widest level (DialFace.slotWords, so the pill never jumps as a level changes) and a five-bar meter in the tier's own heat (EffortMeter.bars, ModelDial.heat — a known tier lights its rank so high is three bars on every model, ultracode lights every bar in the rainbow, the server deciding is five hollow bars, and a model with no levels shows no meter and no word). Hue is who answers and heat is how hard: colour lives on the dot and the bars, never on a word. The pill has two zones: the model half opens the quick pick and the effort half is the effort control. On the desktops the pill opens a popover with two columns from ModelDialState: the models and pinned pairs the person actually reaches for (Pinned, this chat's own model when no pair says it, Recent, the other machines under their own heading, the server's default and the door to the whole catalog) beside the effort ladder (ModelDial.rungs — the power on top, then hottest first, the server's choice last, each rung wearing its digit, its word, what it means in a few words and its bars, headed by how many levels the model takes). The ladder follows the cursor: on the model this chat runs it is the live control, and on any other row it previews that model's own levels with the level the pair would take lit, says what will become of the current level before the row is taken (ModelDialState.carryNotice), and a step there rides with the pick (EffortAsk) instead of changing the chat. A typed query answers from the whole fleet through the chooser's own search, and an empty answer names where it looked and which machine was offline. The axes split the keys: ↑↓ walks models and ⏎ takes the row with the level shown, ←→ and the digits set effort (digits only while the search is empty), ⇥ moves the arrows to the ladder, ⌃S pins the pair, ⌃⏎ opens the catalog, esc closes — and a mouse does the same. The wheel over the closed pill steps the effort one notch (ModelDial.step, pinned at both ends, never wrapped) and ⌃⌥↑/⌃⌥↓ do the same from the editor (KeyAction.effortHotter/effortColder); every client keeps one rule for what a step says (ModelDial.notch): the pill's word and bars moving are the whole answer to anyone who can see them, so a step is never written out as text, and a screen reader is told where the level landed, or that it is already the highest or the lowest the model takes (EffortNotch.spoken), once the hand pauses for 0.4 s (EffortVoice), so a held key is one announcement rather than twelve; only a model that takes no level speaks on screen (ModelDial.headline, the wheel there being inert); ⌃⌥M opens the dial. On iOS the effort half is a rail (EffortRail): one tap on it lifts the ladder out of the pill as a column of rungs over a thick material with the level the chat is at marked, and a level is chosen by tapping it or by putting a finger down on one and sliding before lifting (a soft tick per rung entered, hysteresis rather than a flicker); lifting well off the side or tapping the dimmed page chooses nothing, and sliding along the effort half without opening anything walks the same ladder a notch at a time (EffortScrub: a tick per level, stopped at both ends and with no dead zone past them, never falling onto the server's stop); the control is an adjustable element for VoiceOver; the same chords answer on a hardware keyboard. A level changes under a pick only by ModelEffort.carry — to the nearest cooler level the new model takes, never hotter, and said aloud (EffortCarry.notice) — so a level the model cannot take is never sent and never lost silently. Every server's vocabulary is read onto one scale (EffortVocabulary: none, off and nothink at the floor with minimal, fast with low, think and thinking with medium, deep with high; auto and default are the model deciding, sorted first and drawn hollow like the server's stop), so a carry between Claude's words, OpenAI's, Gemini's and a local model's think/nothink compares tiers and is still never hotter, and the bars, the caption and the colour follow the tier; a word the table has not met keeps the server's order (numbers in numeric order), is placed by where it sits among the model's own levels and carries no invented caption. Every comparison ignores case, and the level that goes out is always the model's own spelling. The desktop quick ask wears the same dial as its aim over QuickAskDefaults' own model and level, so the control is learned once."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelPresets, area: "composer", title: "Pin a model and a level together",
            spec:
                "A model and the level it is worked at are one setting, so what a person pins is the pair (ModelPreset: a model plus PresetEffort — a named level, the server's own choice, or keep, which is the older star on a bare model and which still lives in ModelFavoritesStore so the catalog's star and the list never disagree). The pinned pairs (ModelPresetStore, this device) lead every quick pick under one heading: one tap sets both halves, the pair that matches what the chat runs is marked, and a pair whose model the machine lacks says so rather than vanishing. Pinning is one gesture where the pair is seen — ⌃S in the dial pins the model under the cursor with the level the ladder shows, a swipe on a catalog row or its menu on iOS — and pinning a pair covers the model's bare star while unpinning it takes the star too (ModelPresetStore.toggled). Presets are cycled without opening anything (ModelPresetCycle.step, a ring, and what a step did is said as a toast): a swipe along the pill's model half on iOS, ⌃⌥←/→ (KeyAction.presetNext/presetPrevious) and ⌃⌥1–9 (KeyAction.preset) on the desktops, with a Model menu on the Mac that lists the same chords. A pair is applied as a model pick followed by its level, so the level goes through the same carry as any pick."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .unifiedModelChooser, area: "composer", title: "One selector, machine first",
            spec:
                "Every model on every server is chosen from one searchable surface built on the shared ModelChooser — never a flat menu of the raw catalog. The first choice is the machine: every connected server is a tab (ModelChooser.machines, ⌃1–9, the one the chooser opened from first and preselected), each tab wearing its model count, how many are local, and whether it is answering, and the list under a tab is that one machine's catalog and nothing else's — the old merged alphabet, where the same model appeared once per server with the server as a badge on each, is gone, and so are the standing filters it needed (this server / local). A tab that is not this chat's server says once, above its list, that a pick there starts a new chat on that machine (ModelMachine.consequence) instead of saying it on every row. Under the tab: Yours — the model this chat runs, named rather than implied by a tick down the page, then starred, then recent, deduplicated into one short section with the server's own default closing it, where three headings used to stand; then the models running on the machine's own hardware as a section of their own (they never sit in a family beside a hosted namesake); then the families. A row is a name over what settles which model it is — the door a pick would take with the count of the others (Anthropic +1) and the id when it says more than the name — and wears only what would stop a send or what the whole catalog does not share: the wall in front of it, and capabilities in the quieter register with any capability every row shares dropped by ModelFactPolicy. Provider counts are no longer pills, and effort is read rather than chosen on the row: when the chooser is told its aim (ChooserAim — the level the chat or the next chat holds, ChooserContext — this chat, the next chat or a server's default) every row says the level a pick of it would run at (ModelRowLevel, the same five bars and carry rule as the dial), the level is one strip over the list that acts on the model in use (EffortStripView, EffortScrub — tap or slide, one tick a rung), and the pairs a person has pinned (ModelPreset — a model and a level) are the first section, one row each at its own level, taken as one decision (ModelPick.preset). A row is never cut off: a name, an id, a wall's words and the machine a model runs on wrap onto further lines rather than ending in an ellipsis, the band over the list is measured rather than guessed, and where it would fill the screen at the accessibility text sizes it scrolls away with the list, clipped at the bars. A search asks the shown machine and answers from the others too, under one heading that names the machine on each row, so the tab never hides a model somebody typed the name of; an empty answer names the machine as well as the word. Past ModelChooser.foldFrom hosted models the families fold: the list arrives open only where the model in use lives, each heading is a press (⌃→/⌃←, ⌃⇧ for all) that says how many it holds, and one control at the top opens everything or shuts it again. A wall is the account's rather than the machine's. The door a collapsed row picks is deliberate: the door the chat is already on wins, then the model's own house over a reseller fronting it. A star (ModelFavoritesStore) puts a model in Yours on the machine that runs it and follows it through every section; the quick menu shows ModelChooser.shortlist over the whole fleet and hands the rest to this surface, so the two are one list at two lengths, and offers that road whenever it shows fewer distinct models than the catalog holds (ModelMenuLayout.offersBrowse). In the quick menu another machine's rows sit under that machine (ModelMenuGroup — On <machine>, and whether it is answering), so a tap's consequence is visible before it, and such a tap takes the same road as a pick in this surface: a chat asks before starting a new chat there, and a composer that is not yet a chat is re-aimed — machine, model and level as one decision (ComposerRetarget: the folder kept only where that machine knows it, the level carried onto that machine's catalog, one sentence saying where it now points). A recent or a star is taken through the door it was remembered by (ModelMenuEntry), and a pin through the door the machine actually lists (ModelPresetCycle.resolve: provider and id, the id alone only when the catalog offers it once, and a take-any-id server only for its own house); pins this machine cannot run stay listed, dimmed, naming the machine that runs them. That menu is the person's own reaching and nothing else — what this chat runs, then stars, then recents — and is exactly as short as that record: it is never padded out to menu length with whatever the machines happen to run themselves, which buried the two models somebody uses under six they never picked and listed one model twice when a second server also offered it. Only a device that has picked nothing at all opens on the local models, one per model, because an empty menu teaches nothing. Under the machine's tab the doors are a filter (ModelChooser.doors/setDoor, ⌥1–9, ⌥0 for all): one chip per provider the machine reaches its models through — a subscription, a key, a gateway — wearing how many it runs, drawn only past one door, so a person who pays for Ollama Cloud and for OpenCode Go can read what each will run as one list rather than one folded row at a time; a row under a door names that door alone and a pick goes through it, the summary says which door is showing, an empty answer names the door, and a tab change lets the door go because a door is a fact about the shown machine. A heading's chevron and a row's always point the way the list is actually folded — a supplementary view is not an item and a diffable snapshot never reconfigures it, so every client rewrites what is on screen after a fold."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelCapabilitySurfacing, area: "composer",
            title: "What a model cannot do is never offered",
            spec:
                "A capability is the model's far more often than the server's — one opencode machine fronts a hundred models and half of them cannot see a picture, most take no effort level at all — so every affordance follows the *pick* rather than the connection, and the reading is Core's so three clients cannot disagree about one model. ModelAbilities.resolve narrows the backend's word by the picked model's own only where the model is what reads: files are the backend's word alone, because the agent opens them on the server, so a model that takes no media still has its attach affordance and a menu that says why Photo Library is not offered, while pictures and PDFs follow the model's catalog entry and decide whether they are offered and whether a drop or a paste is accepted; a model the catalog cannot describe is trusted rather than assumed blind, because a server that never published per-model capabilities must not lose its attach affordance over a fact nobody stated, and a pick carrying no door still finds its model by id, which is the shape a conversation reopened on another device holds. ModelEffort.options decides the levels the same way — the model's own variants where the catalog names them, the agent's list only where the model itself is unknown, and an empty variants list is an answer rather than a gap. What a model cannot do is never offered and never explained: the effort control is absent, not a pill reading \"no effort control\", and the chip names no effort word left over from the model that answered last. What is already in hand when the pick changes is dropped out loud (ModelAbilities.dropped) rather than left to fail on the other machine, and where the whole affordance is missing the menu says which of the two refused it (ModelAbilities.unavailableReason) so its absence is a stated fact rather than a control somebody assumes they failed to find. Every composer answers this — the chat's, the quick ask's — on all three clients."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .agentsBoard, area: "activity", title: "One board for every agent",
            spec:
                "What the agents are doing is one board on every desk, read from Core (AgentBoard, built by AgentTower): sessions across every machine as agents, each standing once in the most urgent place it belongs. Needs you leads, a card per ask with the command or the question word for word, how long it has waited, and the answer in place: approve or deny (a backend that asks permissions; the phone asks Face ID or the passcode first, the desktops answer the way their notifications do) or the question's own options, sent through the chat's own send path. Working is a row per session — glyph in the activity tone and motion, title, elapsed, the line of words for what it is doing (AgentLine: Editing Backoff.swift, never a tool's name), the heartbeat strip of the last minute (AgentBeat; a flat strip is a stall you can see) and the machine when there is more than one — with its agents as a tree: each subagent with its todos and its own line, the background task with its age. In the background holds sessions whose turn ended while the machine carries work, and Finished the last hour's endings, unread first, with how long, how many tools and files, or why it failed. The headline says it in a sentence (2 working · 1 needs you). The board watches the running sessions only, at most six (AgentWatch, needs-you first, held ones sticky, an ending listened to for a few seconds), over their own conversations at the lowest refresh priority, and only while it is open; a session past the cap is a listed row that says so (Watching 6 of 9 running) and a machine that stops answering keeps its rows dimmed. Nothing is invented: the listing's own facts for a session nobody watches, the transcript's for one watched, and an unknown stays unknown. It opens from Home's status header and the Working band's header and the Sparx orb on the phone and from the Agents entry (and its orb) on the desktops (Ctrl+Shift+A; the orb's plain click keeps its old action and its secondary one opens the board). The Live Activity keeps opening its chat: its tap is a wire contract with every installed app."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .agentPeek, area: "activity", title: "Peek at one agent",
            spec:
                "An agent opens as a read-only monitor without opening its chat (AgentFacts, from its watched conversation): the title, machine, model and level and elapsed; Doing now in large type; the last steps newest first, each said by AgentLine with when this device first saw it (never how long it took, since a tool call carries no duration); the agents it has out with their todos; the files it has touched; and three meters, context fill, tokens this turn and an API-equivalent estimate marked as one, never a bill. Actions: Open chat, Stop (confirmed; the backend's abort), and Nudge, which queues a message through the chat's own queue. A subagent is read inline and never opened as a chat of its own. A sheet on the phone, an inspector beside the board on the desktops."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .agentsHour, area: "activity", title: "The last hour as lanes",
            spec:
                "Lanes over the last hour, one per session with its agents beneath it (AgentHour, from AgentHourLog): bars in the activity tones, an amber bar while it waited on the person, a mark where a turn ended and how, a now line. It is drawn only from what this device saw: the spans the board was not open for are returned as unseen and hatched, so a quiet gap and a blind one never look alike. Selecting a lane fills the inspector with that agent's peek. Where there is width for it (the Mac, Linux and iPad); the iPhone has the board and the peek."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelParty, area: "composer", title: "Six models you carry",
            spec:
                "The model pill opens the party: six slots, always six, filled or hollow (ModelParty, this device). A slot is a model at a level taken as one decision (a ModelPreset) and a name the person may give it (24 characters, wrapping rather than cutting; empty gives the model's own word back). It is drawn as a card per slot (PartyReading.cards: the name or the model's word, the model and level beneath, the level's bars, the house's colour, the machine when it is not this one, IN USE for the slot the chat runs, dimmed and saying so when its machine does not answer) in a sheet of a custom height on the phone and a popover on the other desks, with the two most recent models not in the party as chips and All models… opening the existing chooser. A tap takes the slot through the same pick as anywhere, and a slot on another machine asks first and carries the conversation as Continue on… does. Edit turns the cards editable in place: rename where the name stands, step the level through the model's own levels on the ball's button, drag a ball onto another to swap, release a slot back to the box, and a hollow slot opens the chooser with ChooserContext.party, where every pick fills the slot. Add to party on any model of the chooser takes the first hollow slot at the level a pick would run at, and with six full asks which slot makes room. The ring a swipe along the pill walks and the chords ⌃⌥1-6 take are the party's slots (ModelParty.ring, slot-true: a chord names a slot, never the nth filled one), and an empty party falls back to the pins exactly as before. The party starts empty on every client, never filled from the pins or stars; a model is in it because the person put it there, and an empty party rings through the pins."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .carryChat, area: "composer", title: "A conversation goes with the model it moves to",
            spec:
                "A pick on another machine, from a chat, no longer starts an empty one. The alert says \"Continue on <machine>?\" and the conversation comes along as a brief (ConversationBrief): the turns before the pick as prose, newest whole and oldest dropped whole inside min(0.4 x the target window, 24k) tokens, an earlier compaction summary or brief heading it, tool output, reasoning and pictures left behind. It is built on the device with no model call and rides in the first message after what the person typed (CarryMark.wrap), so the title, the notifications and the Live Activity read the person's own first line and every backend carries it the same way; the tag and its attributes are a wire contract, added to and never renamed. The composer that will send it wears one chip (the source and, only when turns were dropped, 'n of m') whose x makes it a fresh chat. The second entry point is an assistant answer's own menu, 'Try with another model...', which opens the same chooser in ChooserContext.branch (every machine tab, this one too, says a pick branches the chat from that question) and aims a new chat at the pick with the brief cut before the question and the question in the field, unsent; the original chat is untouched. The first row of a carried chat is one line, 'Continued from <chat>' with the count only when something was left behind (CarryReading.line), which opens the brief as text in the compaction reader. A chat with no settled turn has nothing to carry and keeps 'New chat there'. A pick on the same machine as the chat, from the chooser, still changes the model in place."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelSeam, area: "transcript", title: "The transcript says where the model changed",
            spec:
                "A settled answer whose model word differs from the previous settled answer's draws one flat line of 24 pt or less above it: the new model's tint dot and word, and the old one after it (ModelSwitch.line). The words compared are the pill's own (ModelBadge.word), never ids, so an alias against its resolved id says nothing, effort alone never makes a seam and the first answer has none. ModelSeamTracker reads each settled answer once and a streamed token touches only the answer being written, because a transcript rebuild runs on every token. A seam never crosses backends: that is a carry."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .responseStats, area: "transcript", title: "An answer says what it took",
            spec:
                "A person reading an answer asks three things about it: was that slow, was that expensive, what wrote it. ResponseStats answers them under the turn itself as one quiet strip — output speed, time to first token, elapsed, tokens written, tokens read, estimated cost, and the model with its effort — off by default (ResponseStatsSetting) because a transcript is for reading and a rail of figures under every answer is a tax on the reading it exists to inform. It is deterministic and never measured here: every figure is the server's own arithmetic or a division of two of the server's own numbers, so two devices watching one conversation report the same answer identically and nothing drifts with the network or this process's own scheduling. The rate divides MessageUsage.written and never a total — a turn that read a hundred thousand cached tokens and wrote two hundred did not run fast — and needs enough clock under it to mean anything. Where the server stamped the first output token (MessagePart.startedAt), the wait is split: time to first token is its own figure and the rate is measured over the generation that followed, so a long prompt being read is never billed as the model's slowness; a backend that stamps nothing gets one honest rate over the whole wait and no first-token figure invented for it. The speed's tooltip may add what the model has averaged on this device (ModelSpeedLedger, decayed so provider drift ages out), which is memory, not measurement, and lives only in the detail. What cannot be derived is left out rather than guessed: a turn whose server said nothing about tokens shows its clock and no rate, a turn that said nothing at all draws no strip, and a turn still being written or one that failed draws nothing, because a rate over a partial answer moves under the reader's eye and everything settled in this app holds perfectly still. Money follows the spend doctrine — API-equivalent value, marked an estimate, never a bill. Each client decides only the layout: the same facts, the same order, the platform's symbol or the text glyph, the detail sentence as a tooltip and the whole strip spelled out for a screen reader."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelEffortDisplay, area: "composer", title: "The chip tells the truth",
            spec:
                "The model/effort chip shows what will actually answer: the explicit pick, else the model observed on the last assistant turn, else the session's own record."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .ultracodeAura, area: "composer", title: "Ultracode looks unlocked",
            spec:
                "Picking the ultracode tier, typing the word into the draft, or a turn in flight that was sent with it wraps the composer in the shared animated rainbow aura, and a turn in flight is read from the conversation rather than from having been the client that sent it: Ultracode.turnInvoked finds the word in the running turn's own prompt, which is the same text the server decided on, so a turn summoned on one desk wears the aura on every desk watching it (Ultracode.auraActive over Ultracode.rainbowStops) — special powers visibly on before the prompt is sent — and the effort menu presents ultracode as a power (Ultracode.menuTitle/menuSubtitle), not another level. The rainbow travels around the edge and the glow breathes under it, both on the shared periods (Ultracode.auraTurnSeconds/auraBreathSeconds/auraBreathFloor, or Ultracode.aura(at:) where the client paints its own frames), off a clock rather than a counter so a dropped frame costs a frame and not the rhythm. Reduced motion keeps the aura lit and stops it moving: a power being on is a fact, not a flourish."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .stopTurn, area: "composer", title: "Stop the turn",
            spec: "A visible control cancels the current turn via cancelCurrentTurn."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .statusBand, area: "status", title: "Status band",
            spec:
                "A persistent strip states phase, token estimate, and clock from the shared StatusFacts — the same facts on every platform. StatusFacts is a snapshot of one moment, StatusFacts.readAt, and every duration on the band — the dial, the turn's elapsed, an agent row's clock and its age — is measured against that moment rather than against whatever the clock says when a row happens to be built, so a menu unfolded later cannot disagree with the segment that opened it. Agent rows are held by the turn: their clocks run only while the turn is open on the other machine, because nothing refreshes a subagent's record once this device is idle, offline or dialling. A fact holds its place: each keeps the room of the widest it has been in the conversation (StatusBandSlots, a minimum that a narrow pane still compresses, capped at a share of the band), the leading mark owns a slot as wide as the widest glyph in the vocabulary (StatusBandSlots.leadingGlyphs), and the context fact reserves its ring's room, so a clock, a share or a count changes its own words and moves nothing beside it; a chip that arrives moves what follows it by its own width, once."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sessionSpend, area: "sessions", title: "What this conversation cost",
            spec:
                "The price on the chat's own chrome is the whole conversation's, not the last turn's — a turn's cost is a curiosity, a session's is a fact you act on — and touching it opens the account behind it. The server reads the CLI's own transcript (GET /sessions/:id/spend) for per-turn tokens by tier and prices them; a backend that reports money per message is summed locally instead (SessionSpend(messages:)); a server too old for either leaves the last turn's price where it was. The money is always marked as an estimate with its provenance stated, because a subscription bills a flat fee and the figure is API-equivalent value, never a bill. The panel is the same five sections on every client: the total with turns/each/over/rate/tokens, a bar per turn against the priciest, where the money went across answer / cache written / cache read / fresh input, which model spent it, and the five most expensive turns named by the words that started them. Every number comes from SessionSpend; a client decides only how tall a bar is."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .contextWindow, area: "sessions", title: "How full the context window is",
            spec:
                "The band wears how much of the model's window the conversation holds — the share first, the count after, coloured quiet, attention or danger by how near a compaction is, and drawn as a ring filled that far where the client can draw one. The number is the footprint of the last request the model was sent (ChatMessage.context: what it was handed plus what it wrote), never the turn's bill, because a turn of forty tool calls bills its context forty times; the bridge reads it off the CLI's last API call, opencode's per-step tokens already are it, and a transcript nobody measured is estimated from its own characters and wears a ~. A compaction resets the count to what survived it. The window comes from the server's catalog (ModelInfo.contextWindow), the provider the model runs at choosing between two providers' rows, or from what the model's name is known to hold (ContextWindow.known), and a name nobody knows shows a count with no share rather than an invented ceiling; a window the catalog states is believed as stated and its share is capped at 100%, a provider the catalog lists answers only from its own rows (another provider's window for the same name is never inherited), a plain row is not the row of its [1m] variant, a free tier does not inherit the paid model's window, and only a name's own guess that the fill has outgrown is read as Claude's million-token window. Touching it opens the whole window: a hero ring, the fill banded by what kind of tokens fill it (cache read, cache written, fresh input, the answer), in-use / window / free / model, where the number came from, what to do about it when there is something to do, and one action — Compact — through the client's ordinary preflight. Every word and every threshold is ContextFill's; a client decides only how round a ring is."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .range, area: "status", title: "How far the tokens go",
            spec:
                "A gauge says how full a tank is; the range says whether it will last. Every tank is drawn in one grammar — the solid fill is what has been used, the tick is where an even burn would be by now (how much of the window's time has elapsed), and the hatched tail is where the fill lands at the pace so far (QuotaPace, ContextBurn) — and a line of words appears only when the tail has something to say: a window that will run out a meaningful time before it resets (Wall 19:22, 1h 25m before it resets), a context window with turns to spare in single figures (about 8 turns, at the last five turns' pace), a window that has barely begun says it is too early to tell and states no number, and a reset the provider only estimates makes every figure about. Tone follows where the fill lands, not how full it is: 90% with ten minutes to go is fine, 50% on day two of a week is the story. The chat's facts row keeps the context chip and says the same thing while there is room; when the pace makes it news the ring keeps the fill and the words become turns left, a clock chip appears only while a quota window that governs this chat's model will run out first (QuotaBinding.governs), and a cache chip appears when an idle chat has outlived its prompt cache (the lifetime is the server's, read off the cache writes the CLI made: MessageUsage.cacheLifetime) — all three open one Range sheet: a one-sentence verdict naming the tank that runs out first, the conversation's own window and the windows that govern its model as pace bars (windows on the same provider that govern another model dimmed as not this model), the cache as a countdown that grows into a sentence only inside ten minutes or once lapsed, what is in the window by content with estimated shares scaled to the measured total and the three largest items, the footprint per turn with compactions as pit stops and the next three turns hatched, Compact through its preflight, and the provenance. The usage board draws pace bars in every row; Home says one line when a window is on course to run out and its strip keeps every provider with the tick on its bar; the widgets and the Lock Screen carry the tick and the wall's time; and one notification, Running low, fires once per window per crossing for an account-wide window and is re-armed only after the projection has stayed under 90% for half an hour (RunningLow). A projection is never stated as a fact, nothing here compacts, switches model or holds a send, and a change of verdict is announced once. Every number and word is Core's; a client decides only how a bar is drawn."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .usagePanel, area: "status", title: "Usage details",
            spec:
                "A dedicated surface breaks down quota windows beyond the glanceable gauges. It leads with the tightest window — the one that decides when the next send unlocks — as a hero gauge with its countdown, then every provider's windows as labelled bars with LIVE/CACHED provenance and plan details, and it is the road to the month in numbers. The board is the person's and it holds still: QuotaBoard arranges the cards, and the default arrangement is the person's own order rather than a sort by pressure that moves the cards while they are read — tightest-first and by-name are choices, never the surprise. One row of switches names every provider the account reported and every one the client can only offer (a balance behind a key nobody set), and each is shown or hidden there; a hidden provider leaves the strip and Home too. A card is moved up or down from its own header, which makes the order the person's; the lead can be switched off; and every choice is remembered on the device (QuotaBoardStore, tailscode.quotaBoard). The surface is as wide as a desk allows — a window of its own on the desktops, two cards across, rather than a popover the width of a phone — and a bar fills the card it sits in."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .usageAnalytics, area: "status", title: "The month in numbers",
            spec:
                "A dedicated analytics surface aggregates the whole account, and every model the person runs is in it whichever agent served it. Each connected server reports the ledger its own machine already holds — a Claude bridge prices every transcript turn by turn (GET /analytics); an opencode server keeps a running total on every session record, which is the only ledger it serves at a bearable size (one conversation's messages are megabytes of tool output, a month of them gigabytes), so OpenCodeLedger aggregates the records in one request — and Core's UsageAnalytics merges the servers and generates every word. How much time is being read is the person's choice and it is remembered (UsageWindow, tailscode.usageWindow): week, month, quarter or year, picked in the hero beside the number it qualifies, because a total with no window on it is not a fact. The grain follows from the span rather than being a second question — a day a column up to a month, a week a column for a quarter, a month a column for a year — so the chart is never three hundred and sixty-five pins or seven columns of one week each; the chart's own heading and every bar's name follow the grain, the trend is measured on the days and named in the days it compared, and the week's rhythm is always read off the days themselves so a coarser chart never folds a whole week onto whichever weekday it began. The sections are fixed: the window's total with its per-day rate (singular over one active day, no sentence over none) and trend, a bar per day, week or month with the empty ones present, the week's rhythm Monday-first, the day's 24-hour clock, providers, models, projects, tools, where the money went across the four token tiers with what caching saved, the records (busiest day, priciest conversation and turn, longest turn, streak, subagent runs, compactions), per-machine shares when more than one server answered, and two or three insights. A client decides only how tall a bar is. Money is not the only yardstick, because a model on the server's own GPU costs nothing and still does the work: models and providers rank by the tokens they actually worked through (cache reads excluded, being re-reads of text already counted), a row that cost nothing reads 'Free' beside its tokens rather than '$0', an account that spent nothing at all leads with the work instead of a zero, and what ran on the person's own hardware has a section of its own (UsageAnalytics.LocalWork, titled 'On your own machine'): the work in fresh tokens, its share of the window, what the models wrote against what they read where the ledger separates the two, the turns where a server counted them, a row per local model wearing the codename the catalog gives it (the same name a session row wears, ModelBadge) with its tokens, the speed this device has measured for it once ModelSpeedLedger has a quorum, and the context window the catalog states — only facts that are true, never a guess. When at least half of the window's work ran on a local door the account is local-led: the number is the tokens, the money drops to one dim estimate line under it ('$3.20 of hosted spend, an estimate'), the bars, the week's rhythm and the rankings are measured in tokens, and the page leads with that section and the models; a hosted-led month keeps the money first and shows the section after the models. Every client draws the same tiles and rows and decides only how they sit; the plain text and markdown exports carry the same block. What a server could not measure is declared rather than implied (UsageAnalyticsReport.Coverage): a ledger of session records knows the money, the tokens, the model and the project and nothing about the turns inside, so the turn and tool clauses drop out of the wording rather than reading as zero, the clock and tool sections disappear rather than drawing empty bars, and the surface names the machine whose counts are missing. Money is an estimate and says so; a server too old for the route is named in the surface, never a silent hole in the numbers. The surface shares, and the share is modular and made for X: AnalyticsShare is one package every client reads — plain text and markdown for a paste, a stable filename, a caption for the post and a description of the picture, and a card laid out in pure geometry. The card is a canvas of panels (AnalyticsShare.Card.panels): a hero panel with the total, the window, the trend and the per-day rate, and one tile for each module the person switched on, each tile measured in its own width so every line lands on the same point on every desk and a tile gives up rows before it clips text. ShareFormat picks the canvas — a post of 1600 by 900, the shape X shows whole in a timeline and the default, a square, a 4:5 portrait, and the full-length column the card has always been — and ShareModule the sections: activity, the chart over time, the week (its seven letters are the calendar's own one-letter days in the reader's language, Monday first, written by Core into the block and never typed by a painter), what ran on the person's own machine (the headline, two to four numbers and a row per local model with its codename and measured speed, offered whenever anything ran there and opened on by default in the posters of a local-led account, whose hero then carries the dim hosted-spend estimate beneath its token count and whose caption and description say what share ran on the person's own machine), providers, models, projects, tools, the four token tiers with what caching saved, records and insights. A module the account cannot fill is shown disabled with the reason, never silently absent, and a format that holds fewer modules than were asked for says which it left out (ShareSelection.fit). The choice is remembered per device (tailscode.shareFormat, tailscode.shareModules, tailscode.shareCardStyle) and each format keeps its own modules. A poster opens in the Aurora look and the column in the app's; a stored look always wins. Types for the picture are tabular, the headline is the only element above 100 points, the accent marks one thing in a tile, and the footer says in one line where the numbers came from and that they are an API-equivalent estimate, not a bill, with the coverage and not-counted notes where they apply. The look catalog is the app's own theme (Rosé Pine when the app wears System's invisible colours) plus looks authored for a poster rather than a window, each a canvas falling through a gradient with one accent that carries, every slot published readable on the canvas, its fall and the tiles (CardStyleTests). The share window shows the picture before it leaves, redrawn a moment after any change, with a format picker, a chip for every module, the look picker, Copy caption (at most 280 characters counted the way X counts them, built from the screen's own phrases, no hashtags or links), Copy card, Save and the description as the picture's accessibility text (iOS: ShareCardViewController; Mac: ShareCardPanel; Linux: ShareCardDialog). The choice dresses the card only and changes nothing on the surface behind. A client paints the geometry at 2× or 3× into a PNG and hands the picture with the words to the platform's own share sheet (UIActivityViewController, NSSharingServicePicker, the desktop clipboard and a Save as… on Linux). A model the server cannot price is never called free: only a door on the person's own machine reads Free, a hosted one with no rate reads Unpriced and the models caption names the door, because Ollama Cloud is a subscription and a gateway's free tier is somebody's money. Share is offered only once the ledger has something to say; an empty or still-loading surface has no share control. docs/usage-share.md is the painter contract."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .deepseekBalance, area: "status", title: "DeepSeek API balance",
            spec:
                "DeepSeek models reached through a direct API key are metered by that key's prepaid balance, not by any plan's windows, and the usage surfaces say so in the key's own voice: an optional API key stored with the platform's secret store, read once per refresh from api.deepseek.com/user/balance, and rendered as a balance rather than as quota bars — the total available, split into what was topped up and what was granted, with no invented cap and no reset countdown. An empty balance reads as the wall it is: the provider's card says used up and the chooser marks only DeepSeek models (the billing rule in QuotaBinding.bills), never a neighbour's. No key means no card rather than an error — the surface stays what it was, and the settings row states the key's presence or absence as a fact. The fetch is best-effort on every existing usage path so the widgets and the panel move together."),

        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .ollamaCloudUsage, area: "status", title: "Ollama Cloud usage",
            spec:
                "Ollama models served by ollama.com itself are metered by the account's plan — a session window (about five hours) and a weekly window, each a fraction of the cap — and the usage surfaces read them from the account's own vantage: an optional API key stored with the platform's secret store, read once per refresh from ollama.com/api/usage (an undocumented endpoint, so every failure degrades to absence rather than error), rendered as two account-wide gauges with the plan's four-week cost as a detail and no invented reset. The wall is Ollama Cloud's alone: the billing rule marks only models reached through the ollama-cloud door, never the local ollama server, whose usage is unlimited by definition. No key means no card rather than an error, and the settings row states whether a key is set."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .toasts, area: "status", title: "Transient notices",
            spec: "Short-lived confirmations/errors appear as toasts that never steal focus."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverManagement, area: "servers", title: "Server profiles",
            spec:
                "Add, probe, edit and remove server profiles; HostAddress normalizes anything typed; a password is asked for only once a server says it wants one."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .tailnetDiscovery, area: "servers", title: "The app finds its own servers",
            spec:
                "Setting a machine up is a scan, not a form: the tailnet's own peers are asked on both agent ports through the shared TailnetScanner, and everything that answers is offered as a row that adds itself — the machine's name, what answers there, its version, and whether it will want a password. The wait is drawn rather than spun: TailnetRadar is the shared arithmetic of a sweep that laps at one speed on every desk, each found machine taking a fixed place on the dial from its own name so a rescan puts it back where the reader last saw it, brightest as the arm crosses it and never darker than its resting light. A machine already configured says so instead of offering itself twice, a scan that finds nothing says which peers it asked, and reduced motion draws the same dial at rest with everything on it. Where the tailnet can be read locally it costs nothing to run; where it cannot, the scan asks for the credential it needs and says why. On a phone that credential is a Tailscale API token, and a token expires on its own: TailnetKey remembers when Tailscale refused one and says so beside it (Settings, the scan screen) instead of scanning with it again, KnownMachines keeps the machines last seen so a dead token never stops them being asked, a machine counts as online from Tailscale's own connectedToControl before its last-seen time, a token is checked with Tailscale before it is kept, and TailnetDiscoveryReading words every state of the screen — Tailscale off, no token, a token that stopped working, remembered machines — with the one action that fixes it, so a scan the person was waiting on starts by itself when they come back from turning Tailscale on."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .ompServer, area: "servers", title: "Oh My Pi servers",
            spec:
                "A third server kind alongside opencode and Claude Code: omp-bridge (guitaripod/omp-bridge) drives oh-my-pi over the same wire protocol as claude-bridge, answers /status with agent \"omp\" on port 4099, authenticates as username \"omp\", and serves its own live model catalog at /models. The Kit probes, connects and streams against it unchanged; what each client owes is the road to choose it — a setup card or segment that names Oh My Pi, its icon and label in server rows, and install copy for the bridge."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .openCodeTwoServer, area: "servers", title: "opencode 2 servers",
            spec:
                "opencode 2.0 replaced its whole HTTP surface — every route moved under /api, the records changed shape, the events changed name, and a bundled web UI answers every old path with HTML — and a saved server is a saved address rather than a saved API. So the Kit finds the generation out from the machine (OpenCodeBackend asks /api/info, which only a 2.x server answers, then /global/health, which only a 1.x server answers) and speaks whichever it finds through the same backend a profile has always built; a health check asks again, so a server upgraded under a running client is followed onto its new API. The probe recognises both (ConnectionProbe reads /api/info first), and a 2.x server always wants a password — OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD, or the one it printed when it started — which the diagnosis says instead of promising opencode needs none. What each client owes is to reach every opencode through that one door — it builds its backend from the profile (makeBackend) and never names a generation itself — and to say the password rule where a password is typed: the bridges always want one, opencode 2 always wants one, opencode 1 only if it was set, worded once in Core (ServerPasswordRule) and shown beside the password field on every setup surface rather than behind an optional disclosure."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .connectDiagnosis, area: "servers", title: "Failed probes name their cause",
            spec:
                "A failed probe runs ConnectDiagnosis/PortReachability and offers the one fix; never a raw URLError."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverAccess, area: "servers", title: "A tailnet is a credential",
            spec:
                "Tailscale already authenticated every node with a key and an account, so a bridge on a tailnet admits a device by asking tailscaled who it is (claude-bridge TailnetGate, same-user by default) and a password is one of two keys rather than the only one. The client owes two things. Where a server is explained, it says what let this device in (ServerHealth.access → ServerAccessReading.line): trusted through the tailnet as whom, let in by its password, or open — and says nothing for a server that does not report it, because a guess reads as a fact. Where a server refused, a refusal that names the tailnet (AuthChallenge.tailnetOnly, read from the 401 body) is never written as a password prompt — no field is revealed and the diagnosis sends the person to Tailscale on this device — and a scan row wearing that refusal says its tailnet only rather than wants a password."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .firstRunPlan, area: "servers", title: "First run is one plan",
            spec:
                "A stranger reaches a working first conversation without transcribing an address, minting a password or guessing a port, and the three clients say the same thing in the same order because none of them writes first run: Core's FirstRunPlan owns the rows and their words and FirstRunFlow owns the state behind them. The rows are Tailscale on this device (proven where the platform can read it, and honestly unable to on a sandboxed Mac), the machine, and connected. The machine row offers three ways in, in this order — scan the code the computer printed (phones only), open or paste a link, and I do not have a machine set up yet, which shows the one install command (BridgeInstall.needsPassword decides whether it carries a password: claude-bridge from 1.7.2 trusts the tailnet and carries none, omp-bridge and opencode still do) with Copy and, on a phone, the share sheet, then waits for the machine to appear — and says in one sentence what a machine is. The flow probes the address the moment it has one, names the machine in a failure rather than telling anyone to check a port, reads Claude's sign-in the moment the server is saved and opens the existing sign-in inside first run when it is signed out, and keeps the demo one tap away. An address typed or pasted still works exactly as before; a password is asked for only once a server has said it wants one."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .pairingLink, area: "servers", title: "A pairing link prefills first run",
            spec:
                "The machine prints a tailscode://connect link (PairingKit) and the app opens it: ConnectLink reads it into a PairingLink or the plain reason it is not one (damaged, asks for something else, not a link), ConnectLinkPlan decides what happens — first run pre-selects the agent, fills the host and port and probes at once; a link for a server already added just opens that server; a link arriving when servers exist asks Add this machine? first — and ConnectLinkReading says what the link says, including This address is not on a tailnet when the host cannot be one, in which case nothing is tried until the person goes on. The link only prefills: nothing is saved until Connect, and the password the link may carry (key) goes to the keychain and to the host only after that press, never into a log (PairingLink.redacted). Every client has a way to hand the app a link: iOS opens it from any app and from a scanned code, the Mac registers the tailscode scheme and handles the open, Linux registers x-scheme-handler/tailscode and accepts tailscode --connect <link> or a bare tailscode:// argument on the single instance already running, and the desktops' machine step notices a link on the clipboard and offers it (addAnotherMachine)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .pairingScan, area: "servers", title: "Scan the code the computer printed",
            spec:
                "The install prints a QR code beside its link and a phone reads it with the camera (AVCaptureMetadataOutput, QR only) straight into the same ConnectLink handler a pasted link goes through. A camera that is denied or absent is one plain line and the paste path, never a dead end, and a code that is not a Tailscode link is told so in the link's own words. A desktop has no camera to point at a terminal and answers not applicable: it pastes or opens the link."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverSignIn, area: "servers", title: "Split browser sign-in",
            spec:
                "claude auth login runs on the server's pseudo-terminal; this machine opens the URL and returns the code."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .machinePermissions, area: "servers", title: "A Mac server asks for its grants once",
            spec:
                "A Mac asks a person before a process reads their Documents, Desktop, Downloads or another app's data, on the Mac's own screen, and a bridge is a daemon nobody is watching — so a grant never given is a turn stalled behind a dialog across the room. The bridge reads its own grants without ever raising a prompt (GET /permissions: Full Disk Access, judged by whether a child it starts can open the privacy database) and can open the pane that gives one (POST /permissions/request: System Settings at Privacy & Security → Full Disk Access, with its binary shown in Finder beside it), and it is signed ad hoc under a requirement that names it rather than its bytes, so the grant survives updates. The client owes the checklist apps show on first run, worded once in MachinePermissionReading: where a server is explained, a row per grant — what it is for in the words of what goes wrong without it, on or off, and the one press that opens it on that Mac (Open System Settings when the client is on that same Mac); after the press, the steps — where to look, what to drag or switch, that the screen will notice — while the client asks again every two seconds and replaces them with done the moment the switch is on. First run adds the same step to its checklist when the machine it just reached is a Mac missing a grant, skippable, because nothing breaks without it — the Mac only asks more. A Linux machine, an opencode server and a bridge too old for the route show nothing, never an error."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverSelfUpdate, area: "servers", title: "The app updates the server",
            spec:
                "A server's own screen carries its software card (UpdateCard, drawn embedded) and the press it offers, and every press goes through Core's UpdateDriver — the one engine all three clients share, so asking, pressing and following cannot drift between desks. The bridge reports a job with an identity, a step and an outcome (ServerUpdate.Job: download, build, wait for idle, restart; succeeded, failed or deferred, written by the process that came back after checking it runs the build the job made), and the driver follows exactly that job with every clock named in UpdateFollowTiming: a refused connection during the restart is the restart, a quiet line elsewhere is said to be quiet rather than failed, a relaunch mid-job asks the machine where it got to, and a job another device or the machine's own policy started is picked up the moment a check finds it. The card is steps while it runs — done, under way with its own clock, still to come, or where it stopped — and names what it landed on when it ends. A restart is never taken out from under a running turn: the machine reports what it is doing, the press says beforehand what stopping it would cost, and the bridge holds the restart behind its own quiet barrier. A build that landed and was never started is one press (UpdateInvitation.restartHere), and only where something would start the bridge again; a machine with no supervisor is handed the command. The obstacle in the way of an update is named rather than summarised: the files that are dirty, the commits that cannot be fast-forwarded, the toolchain that would do the building."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverRestart, area: "servers", title: "The app can start a server over",
            spec:
                "Where a server is explained, it can be asked to start over (ServerRestart, RestartableBackend). It is the only way back to anything a long-lived process reads once and never again — the model list opencode resolved at startup, a config edited since, a plugin installed after it began — from the device that is present, on a machine whose terminal is not. The press states what it costs before it is taken, counting the turns this device knows are running there, because a restart stops them where they stand; the restart itself is one command on the machine, left there by the setup script. A server set up by hand is not left with a refusal: the failed restart offers the setup in the same breath (ServerRestart.setup*, ServeManagerBackend.installServeManager) — one press that installs the supervisor, the restart command and the catalog refresher on the machine, idempotently and without a terminal — and the machine that takes the offer restarts when the setup lands. A setup that did not take says so and hands over the command, never a press that pretends. Nothing waits on a reply: the connection dies with the process it was asked over, and the ordinary reconnect is what says the machine is back."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverAutoUpdate, area: "servers", title: "A machine can keep itself current",
            spec:
                "A machine can be trusted to keep itself current, and the trust is the server's rather than this device's: the policy lives on the bridge, off until somebody turns it on, so every client that asks sees the same answer and a phone nobody opens again does not stop the machine updating. A client offers the switch where that server is explained, states in the same breath what the machine will do with it — take an update only when it can finish the job end to end and nothing is running on it, because a restart stops a turn where it stands — and reads back the machine's own account: when it last took one, when it will look again, and what it is holding off for right now (UpdateAutomation, written once in Core). The waiting is a state and not a silence: a build that has landed while a turn is running says so and holds, and a commit that refused to build is not tried again until a newer one exists or the backoff runs out. A server whose policy is on is still shown as behind while it is behind, because it is; it stops holding the standing mark up, since a mark is a request that somebody act and nobody has to, and its offer expires on that machine's own next look rather than standing all morning over a version installed at two. The switch renders from what the machine last answered and says when that was, never from what this device last sent; a bridge too old for the policy has no switch rather than one that does nothing."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .updateCenter, area: "servers", title: "An update is a standing fact",
            spec:
                "Every machine in the picture — this app and each server it talks to — reports what it runs and what it could run, and the answer is one mark the app wears in its own chrome until the update is taken: a chip that says it in a word (UpdateRollup.chip — Update, 2 updates, Updating, Update failed) and opens the update surface. It covers nothing, steals no focus and has no dismiss gesture; setting an offer aside (UpdateLedger.acknowledge) keeps the press and drops the argument for it, and is recorded against that exact offer, so a newer release, a different obstacle or an obstacle that clears speaks again. It renders from UpdateLedger before any check of the launch completes and survives relaunches, and it holds still unless the only thing it says is that something is being installed. Every surface draws Core's UpdateCard and invents no word: the card leads with what is new — the product and both versions in the short form a person reads (VersionLabel: 1.9.2 → 1.10.0), the release notes the bridge reads from its project's own changelog at the head it would move to (or the headline of each commit where a project keeps none), and one press — and keeps every exact number with who reported it in its details, one tap away. UpdateReadings still refuses to call anything current that was not compared: a server that never reached the project, one too old for the route, one that answered nothing, one that built but never restarted, and a store record that has not propagated are different states and none reads 'up to date'. 'Current' expires, 'behind' does not, and an update that landed is news for a day (UpdateOutcome.isNews) — what it became, from what, whether anybody pressed anything, and what came with it. Update everything walks the servers one at a time and never includes this app, which would replace the process doing the watching, nor a machine whose whole remaining job is loading a build it already has."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .newChat, area: "servers", title: "New conversation",
            spec:
                "Starting a chat picks server and working directory in one modal built on NewChatChooser: the folders this device already knows for that server — starred, recent, and the ones its own chats work in — ranked against what is being typed by FuzzyRank, each row saying where it came from, with the typed path always offered as a row of its own so a folder nobody has used yet needs no special gesture. The server last used is pre-chosen, not re-asked. Keyboard-first in two modes: while typing, ⌃n/⌃p walk, tab completes, ⌃s switches server and esc reaches the verbs; in normal mode j/k walk, g/G jump, 1–9 pick outright, i types, f stars, enter starts and esc closes — the grammar written on screen, never a form with unranked buttons under a text field."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .newChatDefaults, area: "servers", title: "A new chat says what it will start with",
            spec:
                "The new-chat surface states, before Start is pressed, which server will host the conversation and which model it will open with. The server is named outright — NewChatChooser.heading carries the machine and agent even when only one is configured — and the model is NewChatDefaults: resolved exactly the way the composer resolves a first turn (the pick this device recorded for that server, else the server's own default, with the effort beside it), re-read every time the server choice changes so switching machines re-labels the chat before it exists. The model wears its family colour and the effort its heat wherever the client already colours chips, a device with no pick says the server decides rather than guessing a name, and the whole fact reads as one sentence for a screen reader. Never a chat whose model is discovered on the first answer."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .newChatPathCompletion, area: "servers",
            title: "The path field is a shell",
            spec:
                "A path typed into the new-chat modal completes against the server's real disk, not this device's memory: the moment the query looks like a path, the chooser names the folder it sits in (NewChatChooser.wantedListing — asked once per folder, because the parent only changes at slash boundaries) and the client answers it over the /files route the file browser already rides, offering every subdirectory whose name the letters find, case-blind, ranked and highlighted by the same FuzzyRank as everything else (PathCompletion.matches). Tab at the top of the list is the shell's tab: the longest continuation every candidate shares, spelled the way the disk spells it, a lone candidate completing whole with its trailing slash so the walk continues without another keystroke (PathCompletion.completed); a cursor deliberately walked onto a row keeps the old meaning and adopts that row. A listing that comes back for a query the person has already left, or from a server no longer chosen, is dropped rather than rendered stale; a folder the server refused is neither offered nor asked for again; and a bridge too old to list says nothing, so the modal degrades to exactly what it was. The rows read live off the disk wear no badge — the remembered ones keep theirs — and Enter on any of them starts the chat there like Enter always did."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverFolderBrowser, area: "servers", title: "Walking another machine's folders",
            spec:
                "Browse the server… in the new-chat modal opens FolderBrowser, one walk for every client and every server: the listing the server already serves over /files (the same route path completion rides), folders only, A to Z, read once per folder and kept so going up or back never asks again. The path is a row of crumbs that each jump back to where they name, with ~ standing for the server's home once a listing has said where that is; up, back, home and the root are one press each; typing filters the folder in view by the same FuzzyRank as everything else and enter opens what the filter narrowed to, a typed absolute or ~ path jumps there outright, and backspace on an empty filter climbs a level. The places a person already works — starred, recent, the folders its own chats are in — are jumps in a sidebar, every folder says how many chats work in it or below it, and the folder on screen can be starred. One verb picks: Start here uses the folder in view, or the one the filter narrowed to, and starts the chat there without a second confirmation. Every server is a keystroke away (⌃s) and remembers where it was left; a half-typed path in the modal opens the browser at the folder it sits in. A folder the server refuses says which and leaves the way up open; a server that cannot list at all says that instead and leaves the typed path as it was. The native picker remains the answer for the machine the client runs on, where it tells the truth."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .newChatFailure, area: "servers", title: "A chat that cannot start says why",
            spec:
                "Starting a chat never fails silently and never prints a raw error. The modal stays up through the mint — it is another machine over a tailnet — showing the working badge while it waits, and on refusal it replaces its own body with the diagnosis: NewChatDiagnosis turns what this device saw plus what the machine answered when asked directly (NewChatWitness.gather, which probes the profile's address and the other agent's default port) into a title, a sentence, and one action. The commonest mistake — a profile aimed at the other agent's port, which answers, refuses, and looks like a dead server — is repaired by the app itself: the fix rewrites the profile's address and retries the same folder on the spot, so the person lands in the chat they asked for. Every other cause names its own remedy (server settings, try again) and no path — no password on this device, no such server, an unreachable host, a healthy server refusing a folder — is allowed to end in a closed sheet with nothing said. The modal also states before the choice which servers did not answer the last listing.",
        ),
    ]
}
